:[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Polyphonic MIDI Output (MIDI Out)","locale":"en"},"1561":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#internal-wiring","content":"Internal wiring","locale":"en"},"1562":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#six-sequences-six-clocks","content":"Metulifer contains **6 sequences** (`numSequences = 6`) displayed as rows.\nEach sequence owns **its own step counter**: they are not locked to one another.\nThat is what makes **polyrhythm** possible: a 7-step sequence can loop while another runs 16 steps.","locale":"en"},"1563":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#six-sequences-six-clocks","content":"One step = a **sixteenth note** (1/16).","locale":"en"},"1564":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#six-sequences-six-clocks","content":"Step duration: `sampleRate × 60 / (BPM × 4)`.\nAt 120 BPM and 44.1 kHz: **5,512.5 samples** (≈ 125 ms).","locale":"en"},"1565":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#six-sequences-six-clocks","content":"Tempo is **global** (`tempo`, 20–300 BPM).","locale":"en"},"1566":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"Each row holds **32 step buttons**:","locale":"en"},"1567":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"**Single Click**: toggles step state (active = amber backlight).","locale":"en"},"1568":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"**Click-and-Drag Paintbrush**: hold the mouse button and sweep across the row horizontally to paint or erase a continuous chain of steps in one smooth motion.","locale":"en"},"1569":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"If the initial step clicked was off, dragging activates every hovered step.","locale":"en"},"1570":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"If the initial step clicked was on (or via right-click), dragging erases steps.","locale":"en"},"1571":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"Continuous interpolation ensures no steps are missed even during very fast mouse sweeps.","locale":"en"},"1572":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"Downbeat accents (steps 1, 5, 9, 13…) feature distinct slate styling while inactive for clear 4/4 grouping.","locale":"en"},"1573":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"The pattern is stored as a **32-bit bitfield** per sequence, inside a `ValueTree`\nserialised with the plugin state — not in host parameters.","locale":"en"},"1574":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#steps--click-and-drag-paintbrush","content":"Storing 192 steps as automatable parameters would pollute the DAW automation lane.\nThe pattern lives in `stepsTree` (properties `p0`…`p5`) and is saved by\n`getStateInformation()`.","locale":"en"},"1575":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#length-and-volume","content":"| Control | Parameter     | Range      | Default |\n| ------- | ------------- | ---------- | ------- |\n| Length  | `seqN_length` | 1–32 steps | 32      |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1576":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#length-and-volume","content":"| Control | Parameter     | Range   | Default |\n| ------- | ------------- | ------- | ------- |\n| Volume  | `seqN_volume` | 0–100 % | 80 %    |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1577":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#length-and-volume","content":"Length sets the sequence's **loop period**: past it, the counter returns to step 0.\nReducing the length to 7 turns a 32-step sequence into a 7-step loop\n(steps 8 to 32 are ignored).","locale":"en"},"1578":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#length-and-volume","content":"These two knobs are **permanent**: they stay attached to their row,\nunlike synth knobs which follow the selection.","locale":"en"},"1579":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#row-buttons-mute-solo-copy-paste-and-arp","content":"Each sequence row provides direct real-time action buttons:","locale":"en"},"1580":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#row-buttons-mute-solo-copy-paste-and-arp","content":"| Button | Action           | Purpose                                                                                                             | Style / Feedback                                                         |\n| ------ | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **M**  | Mute (Electribe) | Instantly mutes step note triggers while letting reverb and delay tails ring out smoothly without any audio clicks. | Bright red backlit LED (`#ef4444`) when active; dims muted sequence row. |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1581":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#row-buttons-mute-solo-copy-paste-and-arp","content":"| Button | Action           | Purpose                                                                                                                             | Style / Feedback                                   |\n| ------ | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| **S**  | Solo (Electribe) | Isolates the selected sequence, silencing all other tracks. Preserves previous mute states and restores them when solo is released. | Bright yellow backlit LED (`#eab308`) when active. |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1582":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#row-buttons-mute-solo-copy-paste-and-arp","content":"| Button | Action | Purpose                                                             | Style / Feedback        |\n| ------ | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| **C**  | Copy   | Copies the entire 32-step pattern to the shared internal clipboard. | Subtle slate backlight. |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1583":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#row-buttons-mute-solo-copy-paste-and-arp","content":"| Button | Action | Purpose                                                                     | Style / Feedback        |\n| ------ | ------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| **P**  | Paste  | Pastes the cached pattern onto the destination row (preserves loop length). | Subtle slate backlight. |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1584":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#row-buttons-mute-solo-copy-paste-and-arp","content":"| Button  | Action      | Purpose                                                                | Style / Feedback                       |\n| ------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| **ARP** | Arpeggiator | Instantly toggles the arpeggiator on/off (lights up in luminous blue). | Luminous cyan (`#38bdf8`) when active. |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1585":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#korg-electribe-emx-1--esx-1-inspired-mute--solo-workflow","content":"Metulifer's mute architecture emulates classic hardware groovebox behavior:","locale":"en"},"1586":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-39","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#korg-electribe-emx-1--esx-1-inspired-mute--solo-workflow","content":"**Non-destructive Mute (*Tail Preservation*)**: Clicking **[M]** does not truncate audio rendering. The DSP engine simply ceases generating new `gateOn` step triggers. Active notes release smoothly, and stereo delay / reverb decays naturally.","locale":"en"},"1587":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-40","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#korg-electribe-emx-1--esx-1-inspired-mute--solo-workflow","content":"**Solo Memory**: Engaging one or multiple **[S]** buttons isolates the soloed tracks. Disengaging solo instantly recovers the prior mute configuration without user intervention.","locale":"en"},"1588":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-41","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#korg-electribe-emx-1--esx-1-inspired-mute--solo-workflow","content":"**DAW Automation**: Automate every track's mute and solo state through host parameters `seq1_mute` … `seq6_mute` and `seq1_solo` … `seq6_solo`.","locale":"en"},"1589":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-42","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#korg-electribe-emx-1--esx-1-inspired-mute--solo-workflow","content":"The row-level **ARP** button allows you to engage melodic variations on the fly during live performance, with detailed chord and algorithm settings configured in the Arpeggiator panel.","locale":"en"},"1590":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-43","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#playhead","content":"During playback, an **orange marker** moves along each row, at that sequence's current step.\nThe marker is refreshed at 60 Hz with zero-latency synchronization by the editor timer (not by the audio thread).","locale":"en"},"1591":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-44","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#playhead","content":"When stopped, markers disappear and counters restart from zero on the next **Play**.","locale":"en"},"1592":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-45","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#selection-and-pagination","content":"Clicking a row **selects** it (blue accent bar on the left) and:","locale":"en"},"1593":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-46","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#selection-and-pagination","content":"re-binds the **synth**, **VCF**, **reverb** and **LFO cables** to that sequence;","locale":"en"},"1594":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-47","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#selection-and-pagination","content":"updates the **display** (`SEQ n`);","locale":"en"},"1595":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-48","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#selection-and-pagination","content":"filters the visible modulation cables.","locale":"en"},"1596":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-49","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#selection-and-pagination","content":"Selection is purely visual: it does not change how the other sequences sound.","locale":"en"},"1597":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-50","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#daw-host-synchronization-host-sync","content":"Metulifer provides two complementary clock modes:","locale":"en"},"1598":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-51","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#daw-host-synchronization-host-sync","content":"**Internal Clock (Free Run)**: The transport engine is controlled via the plugin's **Play/Stop** button and tempo rotary knob (20 to 300 BPM).","locale":"en"},"1599":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-52","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#daw-host-synchronization-host-sync","content":"**Host Synchronization (Host Sync)**: When the **Sync** button in the Clock panel is engaged, the sequencer slave-locks completely to the host Digital Audio Workstation (DAW):","locale":"en"},"1600":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-53","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#daw-host-synchronization-host-sync","content":"**Automatic Start & Stop**: The transport starts and halts in direct synchrony with the DAW timeline playback.","locale":"en"},"1601":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-54","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#daw-host-synchronization-host-sync","content":"**Continuous Tempo Tracking**: Any dynamic tempo changes or automation curves on the DAW tempo track are tracked sample-accurately.","locale":"en"},"1602":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-55","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#daw-host-synchronization-host-sync","content":"**Phase Locking (PPQ)** : The playhead synchronizes to absolute musical time (`ppqPosition`) on sixteenth-note (1/16) boundaries, ensuring that every step aligns perfectly with the DAW project grid even after scrubbing or jumping around the timeline.","locale":"en"},"1603":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-56","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#creative-algorithmic-tools","content":"In addition to manual step toggling and the Drag-and-Paintbrush mode, the sequencer includes three algorithmic pattern engines per track:","locale":"en"},"1604":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-57","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#1-circular-step-shift","content":"Rotates the active steps of a track to the left or to the right while preserving the sequence's loop length.","locale":"en"},"1605":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-58","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#1-circular-step-shift","content":"Shifting right causes steps that exit the end of the active loop to re-emerge at step 0 (circular rotation modulo active loop length).","locale":"en"},"1606":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-59","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#1-circular-step-shift","content":"Perfect for creating syncopated variations and shifting the rhythmic pocket against other sequences.","locale":"en"},"1607":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-60","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#2-euclidean-distribution-bjorklunds-algorithm","content":"Generates a mathematically optimal distribution of `k` pulses across `n` steps as evenly as possible.","locale":"en"},"1608":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-61","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#2-euclidean-distribution-bjorklunds-algorithm","content":"Based on Bjorklund's particle accelerator timing algorithm and found throughout traditional world rhythms (bossa nova, tresillo, rumba, Afro-Cuban bell patterns).","locale":"en"},"1609":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-62","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#2-euclidean-distribution-bjorklunds-algorithm","content":"Instantly turns any row into an hypnotic polyrhythmic groove.","locale":"en"},"1610":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-63","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#3-density-randomizer","content":"Generates a stochastic pattern based on a target density percentage (0% = all steps off, 100% = all steps active).","locale":"en"},"1611":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-64","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#3-density-randomizer","content":"Ideal for fast rhythmic brainstorming and dynamic percussion synthesis.","locale":"en"},"1612":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-65","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Every sequence in Metulifer can transmit sample-accurate MIDI events to external destinations:","locale":"en"},"1613":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-66","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Active steps trigger sample-accurate `noteOn` and `noteOff` events routed to the plugin's MIDI output bus.","locale":"en"},"1614":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-67","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"**Dedicated multi-channel routing**:","locale":"en"},"1615":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-68","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Sequence 1 ➔ MIDI Channel 1","locale":"en"},"1616":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-69","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Sequence 2 ➔ MIDI Channel 2","locale":"en"},"1617":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-70","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Sequence 3 ➔ MIDI Channel 3","locale":"en"},"1618":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-71","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Sequence 4 ➔ MIDI Channel 4","locale":"en"},"1619":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-72","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Sequence 5 ➔ MIDI Channel 5","locale":"en"},"1620":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-73","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Sequence 6 ➔ MIDI Channel 6","locale":"en"},"1621":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-74","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"Allows capturing the sequencer patterns directly into the DAW piano roll or driving external hardware synthesizers and software instruments.","locale":"en"},"1622":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-75","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#polyphonic-midi-output-midi-out","content":"When playback stops, an `allNotesOff` message is dispatched across all 16 MIDI channels to eliminate hanging notes.","locale":"en"},"1623":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer-76","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/sequencer#internal-wiring","content":"The audio thread **never** reads the `ValueTree`: it reads an array of atomic `int`s\n(`patterns[6]`) mirrored by a listener on the message thread.","locale":"en"},"1624":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"page","content":"Synth","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","User guide","User guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth","locale":"en"},"1625":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth","content":"Two VCOs, two ADSRs, dB mix and gain staging — the synth dedicated to each sequence.","locale":"en"},"1626":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#one-synth-per-sequence","content":"One synth per sequence","locale":"en"},"1627":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"The two VCOs","locale":"en"},"1628":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-octave-lever","content":"The octave lever","locale":"en"},"1629":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-note-knob","content":"The Note knob","locale":"en"},"1630":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"The two ADSRs","locale":"en"},"1631":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"Mix and gain staging","locale":"en"},"1632":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"Full voice chain","locale":"en"},"1633":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#one-synth-per-sequence","content":"Every sequence owns **its own `Voice` instance** (`Source/DSP/Voice`).\nImportant consequence: a sequence is **monophonic** — it plays one note at a time.\nThe plugin can therefore produce up to **6 simultaneous notes**, one per sequence.","locale":"en"},"1634":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"| Parameter | IDs                            | Range                        | Default |\n| --------- | ------------------------------ | ---------------------------- | ------- |\n| Octave    | `seqN_octave1`, `seqN_octave2` | −2 … +2 (lever, 5 positions) | 0       |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1635":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"| Parameter | IDs                        | Range          | Default |\n| --------- | -------------------------- | -------------- | ------- |\n| Note      | `seqN_note1`, `seqN_note2` | 0 … 11 (C … B) | 0 (C)   |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1636":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"| Parameter | IDs                        | Range                          | Default |\n| --------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------- |\n| Waveform  | `seqN_wave1`, `seqN_wave2` | Sine / Saw / Square / Triangle | Saw     |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1637":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"Frequency is computed from **C3 = 130.81 Hz**:","locale":"en"},"1638":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"Examples: `octave 0, note 0` → 130.81 Hz (C3) · `octave +1, note 7` → 392.00 Hz (G4).","locale":"en"},"1639":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-vcos","content":"Saw and Square use **polyBLEP**: transitions are smoothed to strongly reduce\nspectral aliasing, without wavetables. Sine and Triangle are computed directly\n(no discontinuity).","locale":"en"},"1640":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-octave-lever","content":"The vertical lever (`Source/UI/Lever`) has **5 positions**: +2, +1, 0, −1, −2.\nIt is bound to `seqN_octaveN` through a `ParameterAttachment`, so it is:","locale":"en"},"1641":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-octave-lever","content":"**automatable** by the DAW;","locale":"en"},"1642":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-octave-lever","content":"**undoable**;","locale":"en"},"1643":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-octave-lever","content":"saved with the plugin state.","locale":"en"},"1644":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-note-knob","content":"12 positions, from C to B. Combined with the lever, it spans 5 octaves × 12 semitones.\nNo MIDI input yet: pitch comes only from these two controls.","locale":"en"},"1645":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"**One ADSR per VCO**: VCO1 → ADSR1, VCO2 → ADSR2.","locale":"en"},"1646":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"| Parameter | IDs                  | Range                   | Default |\n| --------- | -------------------- | ----------------------- | ------- |\n| Attack    | `seqN_a1`, `seqN_a2` | 0 – 5,000 ms (skew 500) | 5 ms    |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1647":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"| Parameter | IDs                  | Range                   | Default |\n| --------- | -------------------- | ----------------------- | ------- |\n| Decay     | `seqN_d1`, `seqN_d2` | 0 – 5,000 ms (skew 500) | 200 ms  |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1648":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"| Parameter | IDs                  | Range     | Default |\n| --------- | -------------------- | --------- | ------- |\n| Sustain   | `seqN_s1`, `seqN_s2` | 0 – 100 % | 70 %    |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1649":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"| Parameter | IDs                  | Range                   | Default |\n| --------- | -------------------- | ----------------------- | ------- |\n| Release   | `seqN_r1`, `seqN_r2` | 0 – 5,000 ms (skew 500) | 300 ms  |","locale":"en","table":"table-1"},"1650":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#the-two-adsrs","content":"Each active step triggers `noteOn()` on the voice; an inactive step triggers `noteOff()`.\nThe release tail keeps ringing after the step, which gives the natural legato between\nclose steps.","locale":"en"},"1651":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"| Parameter | IDs         | Range       | Default |\n| --------- | ----------- | ----------- | ------- |\n| Mix VCO1  | `seqN_mix1` | −60 … +6 dB | 0 dB    |","locale":"en","table":"table-2"},"1652":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"| Parameter | IDs         | Range       | Default |\n| --------- | ----------- | ----------- | ------- |\n| Mix VCO2  | `seqN_mix2` | −60 … +6 dB | 0 dB    |","locale":"en","table":"table-2"},"1653":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"| Parameter | IDs           | Range     | Default |\n| --------- | ------------- | --------- | ------- |\n| Volume    | `seqN_volume` | 0 – 100 % | 80 %    |","locale":"en","table":"table-2"},"1654":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"Three safeguards prevent clipping:","locale":"en"},"1655":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"**Normalised sum**: `(VCO1 × mix1 + VCO2 × mix2) × 0.5`\n→ two oscillators at 0 dB sum to 0 dBFS, not +6 dBFS.","locale":"en"},"1656":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"**Hard mute**: `mix <= −59 dB` ⇒ gain exactly `0.0`.","locale":"en"},"1657":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"**Volume at 0**: the voice mutes its output and resets the reverb (true silence).","locale":"en"},"1658":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"Finally, `processBlock` applies a **global ×0.5 headroom** and a **soft `tanh()`\nsaturation**, guaranteeing an analogue-style ceiling without hard converter clipping.","locale":"en"},"1659":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#mix-and-gain-staging","content":"If the signal still clips in your DAW, lower `seqN_volume` first, then the `mix` values,\ninstead of compensating on the host master.","locale":"en"},"1660":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code              | Detail                                         |\n| ---- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| 1    | `WaveOsc::next()` | generates the sample (polyBLEP for saw/square) |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1661":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code                          | Detail                                          |\n| ---- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| 2    | `ADSR::applyEnvelopeToBuffer` | applies the envelope to the mono scratch buffer |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1662":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code        | Detail             |\n| ---- | ----------- | ------------------ |\n| 3    | `applyGain` | applies the dB mix |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1663":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code      | Detail                  |\n| ---- | --------- | ----------------------- |\n| 4    | sum × 0.5 | normalises the VCO pair |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1664":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-39","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code                     | Detail                  |\n| ---- | ------------------------ | ----------------------- |\n| 5    | `StateVariableTPTFilter` | LPF or HPF (see Filter) |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1665":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-40","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code                | Detail          |\n| ---- | ------------------- | --------------- |\n| 6    | `applyGain(volume)` | sequence volume |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1666":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-41","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code          | Detail     |\n| ---- | ------------- | ---------- |\n| 7    | stereo reverb | see Reverb |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1667":{"id":"/en/docs/guide/synth-42","page_id":"/en/docs/guide/synth","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide/synth#full-voice-chain","content":"| Step | Code      | Detail                      |\n| ---- | --------- | --------------------------- |\n| 8    | `addFrom` | sums into the output buffer |","locale":"en","table":"table-3"},"1668":{"id":"/en/docs","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"page","content":"Metulifer","breadcrumbs":["Documentation"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs","locale":"en"},"1669":{"id":"/en/docs-0","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"A 6-track digital step sequencer with a dedicated synth per sequence — JUCE audio plugin (VST3 / AU / Standalone).","locale":"en"},"1670":{"id":"/en/docs-1","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#what-the-plugin-does","content":"What the plugin does","locale":"en"},"1671":{"id":"/en/docs-2","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#project-status","content":"Project status","locale":"en"},"1672":{"id":"/en/docs-3","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#where-to-start","content":"Where to start","locale":"en"},"1673":{"id":"/en/docs-4","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#install-the-requirements","content":"Install the requirements","locale":"en"},"1674":{"id":"/en/docs-5","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#build-the-plugin","content":"Build the plugin","locale":"en"},"1675":{"id":"/en/docs-6","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#make-a-sequence-sing","content":"Make a sequence sing","locale":"en"},"1676":{"id":"/en/docs-7","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#documentation-conventions","content":"Documentation conventions","locale":"en"},"1677":{"id":"/en/docs-8","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"**Metulifer** is a digital step sequencer built with JUCE for the **Saumure** brand.\nIt packs **six independent programmable sequences**, each with **its own synth**:\ntwo VCOs, two ADSRs, a VCF filter, a 3-in-1 Multi-FX unit (Reverb, Delay, Redux), an 11-mode arpeggiator, and two patchable LFOs.","locale":"en"},"1678":{"id":"/en/docs-9","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"Build the plugin, toggle a few steps and hear your first sound.","locale":"en"},"1679":{"id":"/en/docs-10","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"Processor / Editor, APVTS, sequencer engine and signal flow.","locale":"en"},"1680":{"id":"/en/docs-11","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"All 209 automatable parameters, their ranges and defaults.","locale":"en"},"1681":{"id":"/en/docs-12","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs#what-the-plugin-does","content":"| Block         | Role                                                                                       |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **Sequencer** | 6 sequences of up to 32 steps, with independent length and tempo per sequence (polyrhythm) |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1682":{"id":"/en/docs-13","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs#what-the-plugin-does","content":"| Block     | Role                                                                      |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Synth** | Per sequence: 2 VCOs (sine / saw / square / triangle), 2 ADSRs, mix in dB |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1683":{"id":"/en/docs-14","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs#what-the-plugin-does","content":"| Block           | Role                                                                               |\n| ---